\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The centered symbol and signs under sampling}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{For the centered Fourier symbol of \ref{fdip-000S},
#{f_j(r)=\sin p_j} and #{m=0}. Within any unit cube the
univariate linear interpolant of the two endpoint samples in
coordinate #{j} is affine in the cube coordinates. It agrees
with #{f_j} on every vertex, hence with #{\tilde f_j} throughout
each simplex. A common zero is therefore a product of scalar
zeros. The sampled sine has an increasing zero at #{p_j=0} and
a decreasing one at #{\pi}. For even #{N_j} the latter is a
vertex. For odd #{N_j} the two samples straddling #{\pi} are
opposites and the zero is their midpoint. There are no other
zeros, since each remaining edge has endpoints of the same
strict sign or ends at one of these zeros.}

\p{Near each product zero every component is strictly monotone
in its own coordinate. Each sufficiently small regular probe has
one preimage there. In the simplex containing it, the determinant
in grid coordinates is the product of the one-dimensional
slopes, so the sign is the product of their signs. This also
counts a zero on a face between simplices. At a vertex zero
#{r} the linear criterion applies. For each offset #{w_j=-1,0,1}
in its star,
##{\sin(p_j+2\pi w_j/N_j)=\cos p_j\,\sin(2\pi/N_j)\,w_j.}
The diagonal map in \ref{fdip-0141} is invertible, since
#{N_j\ge3}. Its sign #{\prod_j\cos p_j} agrees with the corner generator signs of \ref{fdip-009R}.}

\p{If #{e} counts the even periods, exactly #{2^e} of the
#{2^n} interpolated zeros are vertices, as in the zero-block count of \ref{fdip-009Q}. With an even period, pairing
the two choices on that axis cancels the vertex signs and also
the signs of the #{2^n-2^e} remaining zeros. On an all-odd grid
the origin is the only vertex zero and has degree #{1}. The
#{2^n-1} off-grid zeros together have degree #{-1} by
\ref{fdip-0140}. For example, with two odd periods the three
off-grid signs are #{-1,-1,+1}.}

\p{For the centered symbol the coordinate derivative signs
agree with these finite signs. To see how sampling can change
a sign even for a finite-range operator, take #{N=8} and #{f(p)=\sin p-\tfrac35\sin(2p)}.
The first and second harmonics use shifts of at most two sites.
The positive neighbor has #{f(\pi/4)=\sqrt2/2-3/5>0}, and the
negative neighbor has the opposite value. The vertex degree at
zero is therefore #{+1} by \ref{fdip-013Z}, although the
derivative of the continuous symbol there is #{1-6/5=-1/5}.}
